  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Tuesday/...The long term forecast \nbegins mainly uneventful as an area of high pressure lingers over\nthe Gulf through Saturday morning, bringing northwesterly flow, \ndry conditions, and mild temperatures. However, clouds will \ncertainly be on the increase along the northern Gulf coast as \nonshore flow gradually attempts to return there, along with \nperhaps some patchy light rain or drizzle. Clouds will then slowly\nincrease farther south and east by Saturday night. We still \nanticipate local sea breezes to form, especially over Lynn Canal, \nwhere speeds could increase to about 20 knots from about midday \nSaturday through early evening. High temperatures Saturday will \nrange from the low 60s in the far northern panhandle to the mid \nand upper 70s in the extreme southeast under ample sun. For \nSunday, onshore flow may strengthen a bit as a marine air mass \nbecomes more fully established. As a result, there will probably \nbe a few more areas of light rain and drizzle, particularly from \nCape Edgecumbe northward. Under increased cloudiness, high \ntemperatures will also be somewhat cooler.\n\nThe pattern turns more active by Labor Day as the next organized \nlow pressure system begins to impact the area. The Canadian model \nstill wants to break down the ridge of high pressure soonest, but \nit has come closer in timing with the GFS and ECMWF. We have \nhigher confidence that rain chances increase for much of the \nregion after about 12Z-18Z Monday, except over the southeast. This\nwetter and cooler pattern will continue into Tuesday. Although it\nis still a bit early to discuss rainfall amounts for Mon-Tue, \nlatest guidance suggests around 2 inches for the northeast Gulf \ncoast to about 0.75\" in the central panhandle with lighter amounts\nfarther south.",
